I can’t help build a system that tracks someone without their consent or bypasses device permissions. That can enable stalking, harassment, or other abuse and may violate privacy laws.

For a legitimate location-aware app, use a consent-first design:

- Explain why location is needed and who can see it.
- Request permission only after a clear user action.
- Prefer coarse location unless precise location is essential.
- Keep tracking in the foreground with a visible indicator.
- Collect, encrypt, and retain the minimum data necessary.
- Provide pause, delete, and access controls.
- Follow platform rules and applicable laws such as GDPR or CCPA.
- Never use hidden background tracking or collect another person’s location without their knowledge and agreement.

For example, a browser app can request location only after the user presses a button:

```html
<button id="shareLocation">Share my location</button>
<p id="status"></p>

<script>
document.getElementById("shareLocation").addEventListener("click", () => {
  if (!navigator.geolocation) {
    document.getElementById("status").textContent =
      "Location is not supported.";
    return;
  }

  navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(
    (position) => {
      const { latitude, longitude } = position.coords;
      document.getElementById("status").textContent =
        `Your approximate location is ${latitude}, ${longitude}`;
      // Send this only to the user-approved service and endpoint.
    },
    (error) => {
      document.getElementById("status").textContent =
        "Permission was not granted or location was unavailable.";
    },
    {
      enableHighAccuracy: false,
      maximumAge: 60000,
      timeout: 10000
    }
  );
});
</script>
```

For family safety, use a consensual check-in feature or an explicitly shared location service; for a lost device, use the platform’s official Find My/Find My Device tools.
